//Destructor

A destructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an object is destroyed.

How to identify it?

A destructor has:

Same name as the class
A ~ before the name
No return type
No parameters

Example:

class Student {
public:
    ~Student() {
        cout << "Object destroyed";
    }
};

Here:

~Student()

is the destructor.

When is it called?
int main() {
    Student s1;

} // s1 is destroyed here

When s1 goes out of scope, its destructor is automatically called.

Why do we use it?

Mainly for cleanup, such as releasing resources held by an object.

For example, dynamically allocated memory, files, connections, etc.

Simple definition:

Destructor = special member function automatically called when an object is destroyed, mainly for cleanup.